Description: Guidance for adding a new feature to the Read Leaf codebase.

Instructions: When implementing a new feature, follow the established Feature-Driven architecture. Break down the task into smaller, manageable steps.

Steps:Create a new directory within lib/features/ for the feature.

Inside the feature directory, create data, domain, and presentation subdirectories.

Define necessary data models in the domain/models/ directory, using freezed and json_serializable where appropriate.

Implement business logic and repositories in the domain/ layer.

Handle data sources (local Hive, remote via Services/Supabase) in the data/ layer.

Internationalization: Identify all user-facing strings required by the new feature. Use the intl package and the generated/l10n.dart file to manage these strings. Ensure new string keys are added and translated.

API Integration: If the feature requires interaction with external APIs (other than core Supabase functions covered by SupabaseService), create or utilize a dedicated Service class (e.g., AnnasArchiveService, GeminiService, etc.) within the data/ layer of the feature or a shared services/ directory. Encapsulate all API call logic within this service, including request/response handling, error management, and data mapping.

Implement state management using the BLoC pattern in the presentation/blocs/ directory.

Develop UI components (screens, widgets) in the presentation/screens/ and presentation/widgets/ directories.

Configure dependency injection for the new feature's components using get_it and injectable.

Integrate error handling using Sentry and consistent UI feedback (e.g., Utils.showErrorSnackBar).

If applicable, consider data synchronization needs and integrate with the Supabase backend and data_sync_strategy.md guidelines.

Reference: Refer to the structure of existing features like auth, characters, library, reader, search, settings for examples.